PAGES=[]
def pg(title,blocks,landscape=False):PAGES.append({'title':title,'blocks':blocks,'landscape':landscape})
def p(s):return ('p',s)
def n(s):return ('note',s)
def h(s):return ('h',s)
def t(headers,rows,widths,size=9.5):return ('table',[headers,rows,widths,size])

pg('Immune evasion evidence and research priorities',[
n('Alexander Young | Metastatic microsatellite-stable rectal cancer | Revised14November2026 | Executive summary'),
p('The strongest integrated research priorities are stromal exclusion and extracellular adenosine metabolism, alongside a still-unresolved antigen-presentation defect in treated tissue. New Caris data overturn the earlier claim of uniformly low antigen-presentation RNA. None of the archival datasets establishes the dominant mechanism in the October2026 cervical node.'),
t(['Hypothesis','Evidence supporting it','Evidence against or unresolved','Most useful next test','Therapeutic research implication'],[
['1 Stromal exclusion','BostonGene TGF-beta/CXCL12 programme; B7 FAP, LRRC15 and matrix proteins.','Radiation fibrosis and normal smooth muscle could contribute; current-node exclusion unmeasured.','Spatial CD8-to-tumour mapping with fibroblast markers and activated SMAD2/3.','TGF-beta or CXCL12 blockade with checkpoint/vaccine approaches, conditional on functional rescue.'],
['2 Adenosine and ENPP1','CD39 RNA98th; CD39/CD73/ENPP1 proteins supported by9/13/11 source-labelled peptides.','Bulk abundance does not establish extracellular enzyme activity or receptor dependence.','ATP/AMP/cGAMP turnover plus immune-killing rescue under selective blockade.','CD73 or A2A/A2B inhibition; ENPP1 inhibitors now merit trial prescreening.'],
['3 Antigen-display restraint','BostonGene IRF1/TAP1/HLA low; PTPN1/2 proteins detected; APC truncations.','Caris IRF1/TAP1/HLA-A/B near median; bulk HLA/B2M/TAP proteins present.','Tumour-specific HLA/B2M and interferon-gamma response, with AC484 comparison.','AC484 remains plausible; APC11 is preclinical. Neither is selected by expression alone.'],
['4 Myeloid suppression','AXL 99th, MERTK 93rd, CSF1 91st; AXL/CD163/CD47/SIRPA proteins.','SPP1-state dominance unproven; weak clinical results for indiscriminate myeloid blockade.','Spatial myeloid states; removal/add-back and phagocytosis/killing experiments.','AXL/MERTK or selected myeloid trials; CD47/EGFR/checkpoint triple has adverse CRC evidence.'],
['5 Useful effectors','MSS/low mutation burden; low blood lymphocytes; non-inflamed resection.','Historical liver contains T/NK cells; presence and clonality do not establish tumour recognition.','Paired single-cell RNA/TCR plus native tumour recognition and blood monitoring.','Vaccine or TCR strategies; N-803 adjunct remains experimental and does not fix every barrier.'],
['6 to8 Secondary axes','COX2 RNA, vascular markers and alternative checkpoint proteins provide leads.','PGE2/hypoxia and receptor engagement unmeasured; protein detection is not dependence.','Measure PGE2, vessel/hypoxia patterns and matching receptor/ligand function.','EP4 or alternative-checkpoint trials remain low priority until current dependency is demonstrated.'],
],[1.45,2.25,2.30,2.35,2.34],9.2),
n('Evidence is separated by specimen in the following tables. The new review supplies a tumour-intrinsic, myeloid and stromal framework; functional bottlenecks can change by lesion and treatment. BOT/BAL means botensilimab (CTLA4 blockade) plus balstilimab (PD1 blockade). Prior exposure limits extrapolation from checkpoint-naive trials. These are research priorities, not treatment orders. [R1] [C15] [P1]')],True)

pg('What changed and which samples were assessed',[
p('This revision replaces the conclusions of the22October2026 report while preserving its original files and analyses. It incorporates the7November Caris technical delivery, the29October quantitative proteomics release, later tissue-preservation and histology clarification, the6November blood counts, and updated therapeutic evidence. The13November review is a narrative synthesis, not a trial or a validated algorithm for selecting combinations. [R1]'),
t(['Dataset and specimen','What it contributes','Limit that changes interpretation'],[
['Caris rectal biopsy\n27March2024','Clinical DNA/IHC, research RNA, and new assay-matched colorectal percentiles for five antigen-presentation genes.','Provider reports30% tumour and QC pass. New results describe this archival biopsy, not the recent node.'],
['Tempus rectal resection\n22October2024','Targeted DNA panel and RNA; prior Salmon re-quantification retained.','30% tumour after microdissection per correspondence; not whole-exome coverage. No new broad clinical expression percentiles.'],
['BostonGene resection B4\n22October2024','Bulk RNA with colorectal percentiles, genomic portrait and inferred microenvironment.','About15% tumour; treated specimen. Same surgical lesion as Tempus, not an independent metastasis.'],
['Valius liver metastasis\n22October2024','Selected single-cell RNA summaries and CEA/other IHC.','Liver bulk sequencing failed QC. Selected summaries are not a full cell-by-gene matrix or spatial map.'],
['Proteomics B7_43\n22October2024 resection','Protein and precursor matrices; patient analytical run422; new immune-axis extraction.','B7 differs from B4; purity and precise section/aliquot chain unresolved. Run 423 and HPA control are not matched patient normals.'],
['Cervical node\n6October2026','Histology confirms metastatic colorectal adenocarcinoma; banked fragments offer potential experiments.','A1 extensive necrosis; A2 viable carcinoma reported, with very focal lymphoid tissue. No new node RNA/proteomic result located.'],
['Blood and research summaries','Caris Assure cell-free/buffy-coat RNA; ctDNA; vaccine candidates; TrufflePig analysis.','Blood RNA is not lesion RNA. TrufflePig/input provenance and selected merged-export columns do not create independent tumour evidence.'],
],[1.65,2.70,2.62],9.4),
n('Collection date and assay/report date are distinct. BostonGene/Tempus tumour percentages must not be assigned to B7 or the node. Sources [C4] [C5] [C13] [C2] [C3] [C6] [C12] [C15] [P1] [C16].'),
p('The6November lymphocyte count was0.8x10^9/L and flagged low. This supports checking current lymphocyte subsets, collection yield and treatment timing; it does not diagnose absent tumour-specific T cells or justify a cytokine treatment by itself. RECIST nonmeasurability is no longer assumed: the user reports3November clinician advice that disease should be measurable, pending trial-site confirmation. [C17]')])

pg('Evidence across datasets for recognition and signalling',[
n('RNA percentiles are within each provider reference. TPM means transcripts per million; research TPM is not a clinical percentile. Proteomics counts below mean source-labelled proteotypic sequences supporting identification, not abundance percentiles. NR means not supplied in the selected dataset, not absent.'),
t(['Mechanism','Caris biopsy','Tempus resection','BostonGene B4','Valius liver','B7 protein evidence','Revised interpretation'],[
['Interferon and antigen presentation','New percentiles: IRF1 53.6; TAP1 46.3; HLA-A/B/C55.0/54.2/29.2.','Research total TPM: IRF1 33.6; TAP1 26.1; HLA-A/B/C439/548/338.','IRF1 2nd; TAP1 1st; HLA-A/B/C2nd/5th/3rd. TAP2 12th; NLRC5 11th; PSMB8/9 1st.','Core gene panel not provided in selected liver summaries.','HLA-A/B/C5/5/4 peptides; B2M 3; TAP1/2 8/11; STAT1 26. No surface or phospho assay.','Low resection RNA remains credible. Universal suppression and complete machinery absence are unsupported; tumour-cell function unresolved.'],
['PTPN1 and PTPN2 signalling restraint','Research TPM31.0/56.3; no established activating mutation.','TPM28.3/29.4; historical DNA panel lacks these genes.','PTPN1 75th, PTPN2 37th. Neither is a validated response biomarker.','NR; cannot infer enzyme activity from other tissue.','PTPN1 11 peptides, PTPN2 3; total protein only.','Targets present in archival bulk tissue. Test incremental rescue by AC484 beyond interferon; immune and tumour effects may differ.'],
['APC and PTPN13','APC truncations at residues450 and1411; PTPN13 research TPM4.87.','APC 450 truncation reported; PTPN13 TPM18.49. Second APC event not listed.','PTPN13 93rd. No measured STAT1 dephosphorylation or functional dependence.','NR; no usable liver bulk sequencing.','PTPN13 not detected; PTPN12 detected with5 peptides.','APC provides a mechanistic fit for APC11 testing, not proof. Non-detection cannot exclude PTPN13; AC484 targets PTPN1/2 instead.'],
['PTEN and fixed HLA loss','PTEN stop at residue335; no HLA-A allele-loss signal reported.','No validated B2M lesion; RNA presence does not prove functional tumour surface protein.','NRF2 RNA97th, KEAP1 12th, NBR1 81st; several NRF2 outputs low.','No validated B2M loss. CEA IHC H-score 300 offers a separate recognition target.','B2M and HLA proteins detected in bulk; no functional PTEN or nuclear NRF2 assessment.','The historical B2M research candidate has weak/read-end-biased support. No fixed loss or active NRF2 degradation is established.'],
],[1.27,1.48,1.48,1.65,1.42,1.65,1.74],8.8),
n('Sources [C4] [C5] [C2] [C3] [C6] [C10] [C12] [C15] [P1] [P2]. B2M is beta-2-microglobulin, a component required for stable HLA class I; its unvalidated candidate is not an established therapeutic target.')],True)

pg('Evidence across datasets for the microenvironment',[
n('These axes can cooperate. A mixed bulk signature cannot identify which compartment is suppressive or which intervention will restore killing. No biological trend is inferred merely by comparing two different archival specimens.'),
t(['Mechanism','Caris biopsy RNA','Tempus resection RNA','BostonGene B4','Valius liver','B7 protein evidence','Meaning and counterevidence'],[
['Fibroblasts and matrix','Research FAP 3.67; CXCL12 7.63; TGFB2 2.46 TPM. Initial canonical subtype.','FAP 73.89; CXCL12 96.50; TGFB2 20.96 TPM.','Fibrotic/non-inflamed; TGFB2/3 98th; CXCL12/ACTA2 99th; FAP 78th.','16040 fibroblasts recovered; FAP RNA3.91% of fibroblasts.','FAP 14, LRRC15 8, CTHRC1 5, COL11A1 2, TGFB1 4 peptides; extensive matrix/smooth-muscle proteins.','Strongest multi-assay archival signal. Radiotherapy fibrosis, mucosa and smooth muscle confound; active exclusion requires spatial proof.'],
['2 Adenosine and ENPP1','CD39/CD73/ENPP1 research TPM15.71/34.94/21.10.','Corresponding TPM80.30/31.79/11.46.','CD39 98th; CD73 63rd; ENPP1 80th; A2A 51st, A2B 8th.','CD73 RNA1.08% malignant,3.52% nonmalignant; CD39 undetected in selected CD4/CD8,4.2% NK.','CD39 9, CD73 13, ENPP1 11 peptides. CD39/CD73 reference rank shifts positive in two CRC cohorts.','Raises priority of extracellular nucleotide testing. Cell-specific sources, enzymatic flux and receptor competence remain unknown.'],
['Myeloid and efferocytosis','AXL 28.6; MERTK 56.2; GAS6 27.6 TPM.','AXL 70.1; MERTK 44.1; GAS6 124.9 TPM.','AXL 99th; MERTK 93rd; CSF1 91st; CD163 84th. SPP1 60th; CXCL8 7th; CD68 14th.','11273 macrophages and6066 neutrophils recovered; no demonstrated suppressive function.','AXL 4, CD163 8, SPP1 2, CSF1R 1, CD47 4, SIRPA 2 peptides.','Broad macrophage/clearance hypothesis is stronger than a specific SPP1-high or CXCR2-neutrophil diagnosis. Counts may include liver-resident cells.'],
['Effector and alternative checkpoints','MSS; TMB5 mutations/Mb.','MSS; TMB4.2 mutations/Mb.','Non-inflamed; TMB0.42 in low purity. CD8A 56th, CD8B 16th, PRF1 26th; HLA-E 36th.','2231 CD8 and1732 NK; CD8 PD1/LAG3/TIGIT RNA16.6/27.8/15.5%; NK TIGIT 45.6%.','HLA-E 2 peptides, unconfirmed HLA-G assignment, galectin-9 4; no quantitative exhaustion or T-cell specificity measurement.','Insufficient priming and inhibition both plausible. Low TMB is not absence of neoantigens; checkpoint expression is not proof of rescue by blockade.'],
],[1.28,1.48,1.48,1.72,1.50,1.68,1.55],8.8),
n('Sources [C2] [C4] [C5] [C6] [C12] [P1] [P2]. Efferocytosis means clearance of dying cells; GAS6 with AXL/MERTK can couple this process to immune restraint. No assay here measures its activity.')],True)

# Numerically generated table prevents transposition of the updated Caris values.
caris=list(csv.DictReader((BASE/'gene_percentiles.csv').open()))
mat={r['gene']:r for r in csv.DictReader((ROOT/'rna/immune_axis_expression_matrix.csv').open())}
rows=[]
for r in caris:
 g=r['gene']; m=mat[g]
 rows.append([g,f"{float(r['query_tpm']):.2f}",f"{float(r['percentile']):.2f}",m['BostonGene_resection_CRC_percentile'],f"{float(m['Caris_primary_research_total_TPM']):.2f} / {float(m['Tempus_resection_research_total_TPM']):.2f}"])
pg('Reconciling the RNA results',[
p('For the archival biopsy, Caris now supplies the most relevant available expression comparator: its own colorectal assay cohort. Four genes lie around the median, while HLA-C is below the median but not in the extreme lower tail. This supersedes the earlier report\'s inference that the biopsy independently confirms globally low antigen-presentation expression. [C15]'),
t(['Gene','Caris provider TPM','Caris CRC percentile','BostonGene CRC percentile','Research TPM Caris / Tempus'],rows,[.67,1.30,1.50,1.50,2.0],9.7),
h('Why the reference comparisons disagree'),
p('The previous analysis quantified Caris, Tempus and18 GSE50760 primary colorectal tumours with Salmon2.8.0 and GENCODE 37. TAP1 and HLA-A/B/C were low relative to that small external series. Those numerical comparisons remain reproducible, but identical software cannot eliminate differences in tissue preservation, RNA enrichment, fragment quality, cell mixture or cohort selection. GSE50760 used poly-A-selected RNA; the assay-matched Caris comparison has much greater relevance for interpreting the Caris biopsy. Neither comparator validates a tumour-cell-specific biological defect. [Q7] [Q8] [Q3]'),
p('The new table uses58,610 comparator cases; the provider narrative says59,608. That unresolved count discrepancy does not explain away the near-median percentile values. Both source values are retained. Caris states30% tumour and QC passed. Provider total TPM differs from our research TPM, especially for IRF1 and HLA-A, so they are not interchangeable measurements. [C15]'),
h('Coding RNA and cell composition remain important'),
p('Caris reports protein-coding shares of11% for IRF1 and21% for TAP1, versus97%,85% and67% for HLA-A/B/C. These are transcript annotation categories, not translated protein fractions or measurements of abnormal splicing. There is no coding-only assay-matched cohort distribution. TAP1 coding estimates disagree: provider4.07TPM/21% versus earlier research12.71TPM/about68% on the same Caris RNA. IRF1 coding estimates agree more closely at19.3 versus18.8TPM. Transcript-reference/assignment differences require reconciliation before biological interpretation. Do not compare coding TPM against a total-RNA reference or infer a functional loss from the fraction alone. [C15]'),
p('BostonGene low IRF1/TAP/HLA and immunoproteasome RNA remains a coherent observation in treated B4 tissue. Low purity and stromal abundance are competing explanations; current evidence cannot distinguish dilution, lack of interferon stimulation, intrinsic repression or subclonal loss. Tempus is useful corroboration from the resection but not an independent longitudinal lesion replicate. No new Tempus assay-matched percentile delivery was found. [C2] [C13] [C14]')])

prot={r['query_gene']:r for r in csv.DictReader((ROOT/'proteomics/immune_axes_proteomics.csv').open())}
prows=[]
for g in ['HLA-A','HLA-B','HLA-C','B2M','TAP1','TAP2','STAT1','PTPN1','PTPN2','FAP','LRRC15','CTHRC1','NT5E','ENTPD1','ENPP1','AXL','CD163','SPP1','HLA-E','HLA-G']:
 r=prot[g];prows.append([g,f"{float(r['intensity']):,.0f}" if r['intensity'] else 'Not quantified',r['n_unique_sequences'] or '0','Non-specific HLA assignment' if g=='HLA-G' else 'One-peptide lead only' if r['n_unique_sequences']=='1' else 'Identification supported; function unknown'])
pg('Protein evidence and its limits',[
p('Fresh extraction checks the immune-axis table against the supplied patient protein-group matrix. It is a reanalysis of processed quantities and precursor support, not a new search of instrument spectra. The listed intensity is an arbitrary instrument-derived quantity; it cannot be compared between proteins as a molecule count. [P1] [P2]'),
t(['Protein or gene','Patient group signal','Supporting sequences','Interpretation'],prows,[1.18,1.53,1.1,3.16],9.1),
n('NT5E encodes CD73; ENTPD1 encodes CD39. These labels describe the same proteins. Counts are distinct sequences labelled proteotypic in the source, not independently guaranteed gene specificity. In the conservative audit, HLA-C retains3/4 and TAP1 7/8 gene-specific sequences; HLA-A/B, B2M, TAP2, STAT1 and PTPN1/2 retain their specificity; HLA-G does not. The database is not personalised for all patient HLA alleles. The sole HLA-G-labelled sequence DYLALNEDLR is not gene-specific under the I/L-aware audit; HLA-G requires independent confirmation.'),
p('The external HPA normal-colon control and unknown run423 are excluded as patient matched normals. Cross-cohort rank shifts are descriptive, not assay-matched CRC percentiles or replicated differential expression. B7 has no measured malignant-cell fraction; bulk HLA can arise from tumour or nonmalignant cells. No phosphoproteomics, surface proteomics or tumour immunopeptidomics was supplied. [P1]'),
n('IRF1 and PTPN13 non-detection does not establish absence. High support for STAT1 does not establish STAT1 phosphorylation. The smooth-muscle programme is represented by other contractile proteins even when ACTA2 is not separately assigned. These distinctions prevent protein presence/absence from being turned into a pathway diagnosis.')])

pg('Distinguishing intrinsic defects from reversible restraint',[
h('A causal interferon experiment is more informative than another baseline score'),
p('PTPN1/2 are expressed at RNA and protein level, but PTPN2 is only37th percentile in BostonGene and neither protein shows clear selective enrichment against all references. AC484 can act on malignant and immune compartments. Its strongest case-specific justification is a testable ability to improve interferon responses or immune function, rather than a demonstrated PTPN-driven tumour. Compare interferon-gamma alone with AC484 alone and the combination, measuring early activated STAT1, later IRF1/TAP RNA, tumour surface HLA and actual immune killing. [C2] [P1] [L1]'),
p('If interferon alone restores display, insufficient local stimulation may be more important than a drug-sensitive phosphatase. If activated STAT1 rises without HLA restoration, look downstream at transcription, processing and trafficking. If HLA increases without killing, the remaining limitation may be antigen specificity, effector function or access. Gene-specific perturbation and rescue are needed to attribute a drug result to PTPN1/2. No result from this experiment establishes clinical safety of a combination.'),
h('APC and PTEN remain hypotheses with different predictions'),
p('APC is a tumour suppressor with a C-terminal region implicated in restraining PTPN13. The two reported truncations may remove that region, but their phase and current-node retention are unresolved. APC11 is an11-amino-acid APC-derived peptide; TAT is its cell-entry tag. The published work is preclinical. Test PTPN13 suppression or APC11 with delivery/inactive-sequence controls, then surface HLA and killing. PTPN13 protein non-detection in B7 neither refutes nor confirms this dependence. AC484 is not a PTPN13 inhibitor. [L3]'),
p('The PTEN stop at residue335 and high NRF2 RNA support examining the proposed PTEN-KEAP1-NRF2 route to HLA degradation. PTEN is detected with two source-labelled peptides, which does not establish an intact protein, its tumour-cell origin or preserved activity. NRF2 protein/nuclear localisation and NBR1-mediated trafficking are unmeasured; several RNA outputs are low. The decisive result is selective restoration of surface HLA and immune killing after mechanistically controlled perturbation. ML385 remains a research probe. [L4] [P1]'),
h('Genetic loss and epigenetic repression should not be assumed'),
p('Beta-2-microglobulin, encoded by B2M, stabilises HLA class I. The earlier B2M cysteine-to-arginine candidate at residue100 is a weak research call with read-end bias, not a validated mutation or target. Confirm any current tumour DNA event with matched normal, copy number and protein localisation before concluding irreversible loss. Bulk HLA/B2M detection does not exclude a deficient malignant subclone.'),
p('Low NLRC5 and immunoproteasome RNA makes epigenetic repression worth testing if proximal interferon signalling is intact but transcription remains deficient. No case methylation or chromatin-accessibility assay proves this. PTPN12 protein is present, but its TRIM28/double-stranded-RNA mechanism is unmeasured. Doxycycline in the PTPN12 study activated an engineered gene-silencing construct; it was not a PTPN12 drug. Broad epigenetic combinations have mixed CRC evidence and no established predictive biomarker here. [L5] [L15] [L16]')])

pg('Stromal exclusion and extracellular nucleotide suppression',[
h('The stromal finding is coherent but its cause and location are unsettled'),
p('FAP, LRRC15, CTHRC1, THBS2 and collagen support a fibroblast/matrix programme in archival B7, alongside high TGF-beta/CXCL12-related RNA in B4. Strong contractile proteins are compatible with contributions from bowel-wall muscle and post-treatment remodelling. The correct test is whether CD8 cells stop at a fibroblast-rich tumour boundary with active TGF-beta signalling, rather than simply whether collagen or FAP is detectable. Bulk matrix abundance cannot distinguish a harmful barrier from tissue repair. [C2] [P1]'),
p('ITGB8 is93rd percentile and ITGAV 92nd in BostonGene, making integrin-mediated activation of latent TGF-beta a more specific additional hypothesis. In contrast, ITGB6 is2nd percentile in BostonGene but72nd in the earlier Caris panel. This heterogeneity argues against assuming one integrin target across lesions. Assay current alpha-v/beta-8 localisation and activated SMAD2/3 before targeting this route.'),
p('Vactosertib with PD-1 blockade, or CXCL12/CXCR4 interventions, could be tested with autologous fibroblasts and immune cells. A tumour-only organoid omits the relevant barrier. Standard culture medium may contain A83-01, already inhibiting TGF-beta signalling and confounding the experiment. FAP-targeting radioligands are a separate cytotoxic strategy requiring contemporary ligand uptake, normal-tissue dosimetry and clinical review; archival FAP does not predict immune restoration. [L7] [L8] [L9] [L10]'),
h('CD39 and CD73 now have stronger joint support'),
p('CD39 converts extracellular ATP/ADP towards AMP; CD73 converts AMP to adenosine, which can suppress immune cells through A2A/A2B receptors. The data now support both enzymes in tissue. An independent conservative sequence audit retained7 gene-specific CD39 peptides and all13 CD73 peptides, strengthening target identification. Neither abundance nor target identification measures the rate of adenosine production. CD39 may also mark useful tumour-reactive T cells; broad depletion is different from enzyme inhibition. [P2] [L11]'),
h('ENPP1 is a distinct new therapeutic lead'),
p('ENPP1 can consume extracellular cGAMP, reducing STING-mediated innate signalling, and contribute AMP that CD73 can convert to adenosine. ENPP1 has11 independently checked gene-specific peptides. STING1, TBK1 and IRF3 are detected with5,13 and4 source-labelled peptides; cGAS RNA is detected, but cGAS protein is not assigned. These observations support testing the pathway, not assuming it is active in the same cells. Host antigen-presenting cells and tumour cells need separate assessment. [P2] [L32]'),
p('Prioritise extracellular ATP/AMP/adenosine and cGAMP-turnover measurements, compartment-resolved ENPP1/CD73/cGAS, and rescue of immune killing. For ENPP1 inhibition, confirm that preserved cGAMP produces STING/TBK1/IRF3 signalling in relevant cells. For adenosine receptor blockade, demonstrate restoration of effector function despite the suppressive metabolite. Different positive results would select different drugs; they do not justify blocking every component.')])

pg('Myeloid suppression and lower priority alternatives',[
h('A macrophage clearance programme is plausible'),
p('AXL 99th, MERTK 93rd, GAS6 80th, MRC1/CD206 91st and CSF1 91st percentiles, with AXL/CD163/MRC1 protein detection, support investigating macrophage recruitment and clearance of dying cells. They do not prove which cells express these proteins or that clearance suppresses immunity. SPP1 RNA is60th percentile and its protein has two peptides; TREM2 RNA is53rd and protein unassigned. The specific SPP1 macrophage niche highlighted in other cancers is therefore a spatial hypothesis here, not an established diagnosis. [C2] [P1]'),
p('Co-localise SPP1/CD163/MRC1 macrophages with FAP/CTHRC1 fibroblasts, tumour boundaries and CD8 cells. Test removal and add-back of autologous myeloid cells, and separate phagocytosis from T-cell killing. Broad macrophage depletion can remove useful antigen-presenting cells; compensatory neutrophil recruitment is another reason to measure multiple compartments. CSF1R and CXCR2 combinations have weak CRC response data, despite persuasive preclinical biology. [L27] [L28]'),
h('CD47 and checkpoint detection do not establish treatment sensitivity'),
p('CD47 and SIRPA are detected, but CD47 RNA is14th percentile and its proteomic reference ranks are not selectively high. Their interaction can inhibit phagocytosis, yet the evorpacept/cetuximab/pembrolizumab CRC study had one response among16 patients and two treatment-related deaths from severe immune syndromes. This is a strong reason not to promote that triple as an empirical add-on. [L30]'),
p('HLA-E has two peptides and36th-percentile RNA; The sole HLA-G-labelled peptide also matches HLA-A/B/C/G/H when leucine/isoleucine ambiguity is allowed; it does not independently confirm HLA-G. Neither establishes the matching NKG2A or LILRB receptor interaction in the current lesion. Galectin-9 is detected, but its reference rank is not elevated; galectin-1/3 are abundant without specific enrichment. Ligand/receptor localisation and selective functional rescue should precede NKG2A, HLA-G, TIGIT or galectin-directed therapy. [P1] [L29]'),
h('Prostaglandins and inflammatory signalling deserve focused secondary tests'),
p('PTGS2, encoding cyclooxygenase-2, is95th percentile; the PGE2 receptor PTGER4/EP4 is66th. This supports measuring PGE2 and testing EP4-dependent immune rescue. It is not a biomarker for routine celecoxib. Prior ulcer bleeding makes empirical NSAID use particularly unattractive. COX2 and EP4 proteins are not assigned; integrin alpha-v is detected but beta-8 is not assigned. IL6ST and STAT3 proteins are present, without a phosphorylation assay. IL6R/IL6ST/STAT3 RNA is high, whereas LIF itself is9th percentile despite a high receptor; assess ligand, cell origin and activated STAT3 before proposing cytokine blockade. Broad JAK inhibition may suppress the interferon response being sought. [C2] [L35]'),
p('Hypoxia, glycolysis, complement and tryptophan metabolism remain lower-priority explanations. LDHA protein is abundant but not unusually ranked against CRC references; HIF1A/VEGFA/CA9 are not assigned, while SLC2A1 is detected. Complement proteins may come from blood and do not show activation. No lactate, oxygen, kynurenine or receptor-dependence measurement establishes a dominant metabolic pathway. TrufflePig scores do not overcome unresolved input provenance. [P1] [C7]')])

pg('Therapeutic possibilities and combination evidence',[
n('Priority here reflects mechanistic fit and usefulness of further evaluation, not a predicted response rate. No exact multi-drug cocktail has been validated for this case. ORR means objective response rate; PFS/OS mean progression-free/overall survival.'),
t(['Approach','Human evidence','Case interpretation and combination limits','Useful next step'],[
['ENPP1 inhibition\nNew concrete lead','RBS2418 phase1a/b sponsor update:19CRC,17MSS; mixed monotherapy/combination cohorts. Randomized phase2a VISTA-1 recruiting. [L31] [T16]','11 ENPP1 peptides plus cGAS RNA and downstream protein machinery provide a rationale. Sponsor-reported survival is uncontrolled; no personal-response estimate. Vizenpistat+BOT/BAL trial excludes prior checkpoint therapy. [T13]','Discuss RBS2418 trial prescreen and central biomarker requirements. Do not infer eligibility, replace beneficial therapy or create progression to meet criteria.'],
['Adenosine blockade with immune therapy','ARC-9 etrumadenant/zimberelimab/FOLFOX/bevacizumab: OS19.7 vs9.5months; PFS6.2 vs2.1 vsregorafenib. Anti-CD73 COLUMBIA-1 did not improve PFS. [L12] [L33]','Stronger CD39/CD73 tissue evidence; modest/low receptor RNA does not exclude immune-cell signalling. ARC-9 cannot isolate the adenosine drug and excluded prior checkpoint therapy. Development paused.','Measure flux/rescue; seek an appropriate active trial. Do not copy the four-drug regimen or extrapolate pancreatic quemliclustat results to CRC.'],
['TGF-beta blockade with PD1 and vaccine','Vactosertib/pembrolizumab13/103 CRC responses; medianPFS1.2months; prior ICI excluded. QuICC multi-agent CRC experience mostly negative. [L8] [L26]','Stromal RNA/protein supports testing. Vaccine may supply recognition, PD1 may relieve inhibition, TGF-beta inhibition may improve access. Addition of N-803 or BOT/BAL has unproven incremental benefit.','Demonstrate active stromal barrier and rescue in a CAF/immune-preserving model; assess a formal protocol and safety review.'],
['AC484 with PD1 or priming','Phase1 ongoing; preliminary responses reported in previously PD1-treated renal cancer, not established CRC efficacy. [L2] [T1]','PTPN1/2 proteins support target presence, not dependence. Normal archival Caris antigen RNA weakens universal rescue rationale. Adding BOT/BAL is untested.','Compare interferon with/without AC484 and native killing. Reconcile sponsor no-EAP policy with older registry EAP listing. [T11]'],
['AXL or vascular/myeloid modulation','Zanzalintinib/atezolizumab phase3 OS10.9 vs9.4months; broad kinase effects. Prior PD1/PDL1 excluded. [L13] [L14]','AXL RNA/protein is a lead; not an activity biomarker. BA3011 is an AXL-targeted ADC, not proof of immunologic AXL blockade. Vascular benefit cannot be inferred from VEGFB RNA.','Current-node membrane AXL if considering target-directed agents. Review prior treatment, regulatory status and combination-specific evidence.'],
['Vaccine or TCR cells with selected support','Early human CRC TCR proof of concept; N-803/vaccine/PD1 trials remain experimental. [L18] [T14]','Need retained antigen, native tumour recognition, HLA and access. Repeat BOT/BAL benefit after prior exposure is unknown. Low lymphocytes alone does not select N-803.','Paired tumour/blood functional monitoring. If true HLA loss is confirmed, evaluate CEA-directed engagement or cells using current membrane IHC.'],
['Lower-priority empiric combinations','CXCR2+pembro1/40; CSF1R+pembro2/41; NKG2A+durva3/39; CD47/cetuximab/pembro1/16 with serious fatal toxicity. [L27] [L28] [L29] [L30]','Do not stack inhibitors solely because their targets appear in a review or in bulk data. Negative CRC findings must offset attractive mechanism diagrams.','Only pursue when a specific current dependency and an appropriate trial outweigh these counterexamples.'],
],[1.60,3.00,3.50,2.59],9.0),
n('A single additional mechanism should earn inclusion through a discriminating experiment or clinical trial. The exact vactosertib/vaccine/N-803/PD1 regimen, its BOT/BAL substitution, and additions of cetuximab or bevacizumab remain unvalidated. Bevacizumab plus EGFR blockade is not a default combination given adverse randomized CRC experience. [L34]')],True)

pg('Interpreting the new ENPP1 opportunity',[
p('The most specific new drug-development lead is RBS2418. VISTA-1, NCT06824064, is a recruiting randomized phase2a trial, with the registry updated5November2026. It compares RBS2418 plus best supportive care with placebo plus best supportive care within ENPP1/cGAS-positive and negative biomarker strata. It is a trial of an unresolved hypothesis, not established ENPP1-directed CRC treatment. [T16]'),
p('The public criteria require advanced progressive CRC, prior standard therapies received or unavailable through specified circumstances, and progression documented on two scans within2-4months before initiation. They request pretreatment tissue and specify treatment washout/recovery. The abbreviated public criteria show no blanket exclusion for prior checkpoint therapy, but this does not establish full eligibility or remove local RECIST requirements. Current benefit from irinotecan/panitumumab must not be relabelled progression for trial matching. [T16] [C18]'),
p('The phase1 NCT05270213 record lists UCLA and provides another prescreen route, with cohort availability needing confirmation. The sponsor\'s26October ESMO update describes81 subjects overall, including19 CRC patients,17 MSS. Its 15 biomarker-positive CRC patients had a reported median OS of14months, but there was no randomized comparator, and regimens were mixed. That number cannot estimate benefit for this patient or establish synergy with pembrolizumab or FOLFIRI. [L31] [T12]'),
p('ENPP1 and cGAS must be assessed using the study\'s biomarker method. Detection of ENPP1 protein in B7 and cGAS RNA in another bulk assay is not proof of current-node co-expression or a positive central test. Host STING competence also matters. A modest transcript percentile or protein non-detection in bulk cannot substitute for functional and compartment-specific assessment.'),
h('Why the BOT and BAL combination is not an immediate route'),
p('Vizenpistat/SR-8541A is another ENPP1 inhibitor in a trial with botensilimab and balstilimab. NCT06589440 specifically excludes prior PD1/PDL1 or CTLA4 treatment, making the reported BOT/BAL history a direct protocol obstacle. Its existence supports the scientific rationale for studying the combination, not access or a reason to reproduce it outside a suitable protocol. [T13]'),
h('How this changes the research sequence'),
p('Add ENPP1/CD73/cGAS localisation and extracellular cGAMP/adenosine testing to the first focused mechanistic panel. If the current node retains ENPP1 with suppressive nucleotide flux and immune rescue under inhibition, this would strengthen a trial discussion. If it lacks ENPP1 or downstream signalling fails, deprioritise the route despite the archival protein signal. Preserve tissue for study-specific central review before consuming it in exploratory screens.')])

pg('Experiments that distinguish the competing hypotheses',[
p('The first decision is whether sufficient viable current tumour and preserved immune/stromal cells are available. The October node bank contains14 reported vials/60 fragments in90% human serum/10% DMSO, but remaining inventory, per-vial tumour content and post-thaw recovery have not been established. Histology A2 may offer an FFPE route for localisation, while A1 is extensively necrotic. No tissue release or assay order is implied by this report. [C9] [C16]'),
t(['Question','Discriminating experiment','Result that would change priority'],[
['Can tumour be seen by T cells?','Pan-cytokeratin-resolved surface HLA-ABC/B2M; current DNA/RNA; interferon-gamma +/-AC484; total versus surface protein.','Rescue by interferon weakens fixed loss. Additional AC484 rescue supports drug-sensitive restraint. Retained HLA without killing redirects attention to specificity or suppression.'],
['Are T cells physically excluded?','Spatial CD8, tumour nests, FAP/LRRC15/CTHRC1, collagen, pSMAD2/3 and CXCL12. Cancer-associated fibroblast (CAF) migration/killing assay.','Boundary accumulation plus improved entry/killing under TGF-beta or CXCL12 blockade supports exclusion. Sparse cells everywhere suggests priming/recruitment instead.'],
['Is extracellular nucleotide signalling suppressive?','Compartment-resolved CD39/CD73/ENPP1/cGAS; ATP/AMP/adenosine and cGAMP turnover; receptor/enzyme-specific perturbations.','Biochemical target engagement plus immune rescue supports the corresponding axis. Protein presence without flux/rescue weakens it.'],
['Are macrophages suppressing or helping?','Spatial and single-cell states; autologous myeloid removal/add-back; phagocytosis and T-cell-killing readouts.','Removing myeloid cells restores killing and add-back suppresses it. Loss of killing on removal instead warns against depletion.'],
['Are there useful but inhibited lymphocytes?','Paired tumour/blood RNA/TCR; receptor reconstruction or clone expansion; native tumour recognition with HLA controls.','Tumour-reactive clones present but dysfunctional supports functional rescue. Failure to demonstrate useful clones after adequate sampling and validated testing supports priming or engineered-receptor approaches.'],
],[1.5,2.85,2.62],9.3),
h('Keep the experiment interpretable'),
p('Use vehicle and viability controls, responsive positive controls and a small factorial comparison. For example, after confirming an adenosine effect, compare checkpoint alone, adenosine blockade alone, both and neither; assess genuine incremental tumour killing rather than cytokines alone. If both interventions fail, add a third mechanism only when an independent result identifies it. Genetic perturbation/rescue helps separate on-target effects from toxicity.'),
p('Use immune/stroma-preserving explants or co-cultures for microenvironmental hypotheses. Tumour-only viability screens cannot rank checkpoint, TGF-beta or adenosine combinations reliably. Include matched PBMCs where appropriate, document freezing/thawing losses, avoid medium-borne TGF-beta inhibitors when testing that pathway, and protect an untouched reserve for clinical/pathology or cell-therapy needs. Ex vivo response is research evidence, not a validated prediction of clinical benefit. [L22]')])

pg('What paired single cell RNA and TCR sequencing would add',[
p('Paired single-cell RNA and T-cell receptor (TCR) alpha/beta sequencing of the recent node could connect T-cell state with receptor clonotype while profiling malignant, fibroblast and myeloid compartments. It is most useful with a small matched blood sample, current histology and a spatial protein panel. It cannot by itself identify the dominant causal mechanism. [L23]'),
t(['Competing explanations','What RNA and paired TCR can distinguish','What still needs an orthogonal test'],[
['Few T cells versus many ineffective T cells','Recover effector, cycling, progenitor-like and dysfunctional states; assess clone expansion and overlap with blood.','Dissociation/freeze bias affects cell counts; TCR expansion does not prove tumour specificity.'],
['Poor recognition versus inhibited recognition','Assign HLA/TAP/IFN programmes to malignant cells; link candidate reactive T-cell states to paired receptors.','Reconstruct/expand receptors and test native tumour, not only peptide-pulsed targets; validate membrane HLA.'],
['Stromal versus myeloid barriers','Resolve fibroblast states and SPP1/MRC1/AXL/MERTK-associated myeloid states; identify candidate ligand/receptor pairs.','RNA-based communication inference is associative; spatial proximity and removal/add-back are needed.'],
['Adenosine versus ENPP1 and STING restraint','Locate ENTPD1/NT5E/ENPP1/CGAS/STING1 transcripts by compartment, where captured.','Dropout is common; enzymatic flux and STING phosphorylation/functional rescue remain necessary.'],
['Vaccine-relevant repertoire versus bystanders','Compare dominant paired alpha/beta receptors with candidate antigen-reactive cells after stimulation.','Viral/bystander clones may expand; peptide response without native tumour killing is insufficient.'],
],[1.52,2.80,2.65],9.4),
p('Alpha/beta TCR capture does not cover natural killer (NK) cell recognition or fully characterise gamma/delta T-cell receptors. Dissociated cells lose location, so single-cell data alone cannot distinguish a margin-trapped T cell from one inside a malignant nest. Malignant-cell assignment should integrate epithelial markers with somatic/copy-number information rather than rely on one marker.'),
p('After cryopreservation, a feasibility aliquot should establish viable-cell yield, malignant fraction and immune/stromal retention before committing the bank. Single-nucleus RNA may rescue expression profiling when intact-cell recovery is poor, but does not provide an equivalent paired-TCR assay or viable functional culture. Do not consume the entire bank merely to obtain a larger cell count.'),
p('The most useful composite endpoint is a perturbation that improves native tumour killing while preserving viable effector cells, with evidence that the proposed pathway was engaged. That directly separates a suppressive dependency from an associated RNA signature.')])

pg('Analysis provenance and corrections to the prior report',[
h('What was reanalysed'),
p('The accompanying scripts re-extract a broad immune-axis panel from existing Caris/Tempus Salmon gene estimates, the BostonGene/Kandinsky export, selected Valius cell-type summaries and the supplied proteomic matrices. They retain original units, missingness, aliases, specimen roles and source hashes. They do not repeat raw-read alignment, call new clinical mutations or re-search mass-spectrometry spectra. GSE50760 remains an 18-person external comparison rather than a validated assay-matched reference.'),
p('The proteomics panel covers279 queried genes;168 table-listed gene rows match source quantities and peptide recounts. The RNA extraction covers337 canonical genes. The proteomics audit independently checks group quantities against patient run422, recounts source-labelled proteotypic sequences, and tests selected newly emphasised peptides against the delivered20,422-entry Swiss-Prot database while allowing leucine/isoleucine indistinguishability. The expanded specificity audit covers165 sequences from21 targets. CD39 retains7 of9 gene-specific sequences, CTHRC1 3 of5, CD47 3 of4, HLA-C 3 of4 and TAP1 7 of8; CD73 and ENPP1 retain all13 and11. A separate HLA audit finds that the source-labelled HLA-G peptide is not gene-specific; HLA-E retains two specific sequences. This strengthens identification, not activity or cell localisation. Full-spectrum FDR was not reconstructed. [P2]'),
h('Corrections that materially affect interpretation'),
p('First, the new assay-matched Caris results supersede the assertion of consistently low antigen-presentation RNA across both archival rectal samples. Second, the old Caris36th-percentile entry was TGFBI, a TGF-beta-induced protein, not TGFB1, the ligand; that ligand percentile is removed. Third, proteomics B7 is the archival treated rectal resection, not the current node. Fourth, B2M cysteine100-to-arginine remains unvalidated. Fifth, previous assumed RECIST nonmeasurability has been superseded by the reported clinician assessment. [C15] [C4] [P1]'),
p('The historical clinical DNA reports support RAS/BRAF-wild-type disease, not current sensitivity after EGFR therapy. No newly validated disabling JAK/interferon-receptor lesion or biallelic B2M loss is established. Research IFNGR1, HLA-DQB1, SMAD4 and SMARCA4 candidates do not establish functional immune escape. The14-row portal variant export omits major clinical drivers; its omissions cannot overturn the clinical reports. BostonGene low purity limited its clinical copy-number/MSI interpretation, so gene-explorer columns must not override that qualification. [C4] [C5] [C3] The old additional_Caris expression filename remains in the source archive, but its RNA is now classified as the provider-confirmed buffy-coat fraction of the January2026 blood case. It is not extra tumour RNA. The separate cell-free RNA also cannot establish lesion-specific expression. Merged Valius/BostonGene columns and TrufflePig scores retain their provenance qualifications.'),
h('Evidence coverage and boundaries'),
p('The review used release1.67 plus local primary reports and later documented tissue/access updates. No newly delivered current-node sequencing, Olink assay or later distinct proteome was located in that inventory; a targeted provider-email check after6November found the already-incorporated Caris delivery. This is an inventory limitation, not proof that no external laboratory has generated further results. The prior report remains archived; the current revision and analytical receipts are dated14November2026.'),
p('Experimental options should be evaluated against current disease status and the benefit/toxicity of the existing regimen. Prior BOT/BAL exposure is relevant to trial exclusions and evidence applicability. Vaccine manufacture does not establish dosing or tumour-specific immunity. No treatment change, clinical access approval, tissue allocation, email or public website update is performed by this report. [C18]')])

